      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the smallest possible degree of a polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\ldots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to consider the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients. Specifically, if a polynomial with rational coefficients has a root that is an irrational number, its conjugate must also be a root. This is because the minimal polynomial over the rationals of an irrational number will contain the conjugate as a root.\n\nTherefore, each of the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\ldots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) must have its conjugate root \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\ldots, 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots. Since there are 1000 such pairs of roots, the polynomial must have at least \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\) roots.\n\nThus, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
