      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, let's analyze the roots given and understand the nature of the polynomial.\n\nThe roots provided are:\n\\[1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\]\n\nFor these numbers to be roots of a polynomial with rational coefficients, the conjugates must also be roots of the same polynomial. Therefore, we need the complements:\n\\[(1-\\sqrt{2}), \\; (2-\\sqrt{3}), \\; (3-\\sqrt{4}), \\; \\dots, \\; (1000-\\sqrt{1001})\\]\n\nSo, the complete set of roots for any polynomial with rational coefficients must include:\n\\[1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\]\n\\[(1-\\sqrt{2}), \\; (2-\\sqrt{3}), \\; (3-\\sqrt{4}), \\; \\dots, \\; (1000-\\sqrt{1001})\\]\n\nFor the polynomial to have the smallest possible degree, we need to sum all these pairs. The polynomial will have roots that are each a sum of neighboring pairs from the list.\n\nLet's count the number of terms: there are \\(1000\\) terms with conjugate pairs, so we have:\n\\[ (500 \\text{ pairs}) \\times 2 \\text{ terms per pair} = 1000 \\text{ terms} \\]\n\nNext, for each term \\(n + \\sqrt{n+1}\\) and its conjugate \\(n - \\sqrt{n+1}\\), we can represent each term as following the general form \\(a_n + b_nx\\) where \\(a_n = n\\) and \\(b_n = \\pm 1\\).\n\nThus, each term can be represented in such a way that we can form a polynomial of degree \\(1000\\) with rational coefficients.\n\nThe polynomial for each pair:\n\\[ (n + \\sqrt{1+n}) \\]\n\\[ (n + \\sqrt{2+n}) \\]\n\\[ (n + \\sqrt{3+n}) \\]\n\\[ \\dots \\]\n\\[ (n + \\sqrt{1000+n}) \\]\n\nEach term can be combined as typically representing each root multiplicatively:\n\nSumming these, we derive a single term rather more like a companion matrix approach:\nThus, the degree of the final polynomial is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2000} \\]\n\nThis final resulting polynomial with rational coefficients will have the smallest degree you have asked for,\nsince the complete set of 2000 integer linear combinations of perfectly rational conjugate pairs achieves this.",
