      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "This polynomial has all of the numbers \\( 1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001} \\) as roots. \n\nBecause the polynomial has rational coefficients, if \\( a + \\sqrt{b} \\) is a root, then \\( a - \\sqrt{b} \\) must also be a root. Thus, since the roots given are \\( n + \\sqrt{n+1} \\) for \\( n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 1000 \\), the polynomial must also have \\( n - \\sqrt{n+1} \\) as a root. \n\nTherefore, if there are \\( 1000 \\) roots given, there must also be \\( 1000 \\) roots of the form \\( n - \\sqrt{n+1} \\). With \\( 1000 \\) given roots, we must include all possible pairs \\( (n, n+1) \\). However, \\( n = 1000 \\) already gives us one root and the corresponding \\(\\sqrt{1001}\\). \n\nSo essentially, the polynomial must work for roots \\( \\{ 1+ \\sqrt{2}, 1- \\sqrt{2}, 2+ \\sqrt{3}, 2- \\sqrt{3}, \\ldots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}, 1000- \\sqrt{1001} \\} \\).\n\nSince the polynomial must have \\( 1000 \\) roots exactly, the total number of roots is \\( 2 \\times 1000 = 2000 \\). Thus, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\( \\boxed{2000} \\).",
